How do automated HR tracking systems (ATS) and recruiters treat candidates with 5 years experience but no degree versus degree holders when applying for mid-level roles?

When applying for mid-level roles (typically 3 to 6 years of experience required), modern Applicant Tracking Systems (ATS) and human recruiters treat non-degree candidates with 5 years of solid experience in very distinct ways depending on company size, industry culture, and ATS setup.

1. How Automated Tracking Systems (ATS) Filter Applications
Modern ATS platforms (e.g., Workday, Greenhouse, Lever, Taleo) do not "think" on their own; they follow screening logic set up by HR.

A. The "Knockout Question" Gate (Highest Risk)
How it works: When applying, you are often asked a boolean question: "Do you possess a Bachelor's Degree or higher? (Yes/No)"

The Treatment: If HR sets this as a hard knockout rule, the ATS will automatically archive the application or send an automated rejection email—regardless of what is written on the resume.

Where this happens most: Large enterprise corporations, legacy multinational firms, financial institutions, and government/public sector roles where compliance policies mandate strict credential minimums.

B. Semantic & Keyword Parsing (Moderate Risk)
How it works: ATS parsers extract work history, job titles, and skills, matching them against the job description.   
Hyring

The Treatment: Newer ATS platforms use semantic search. If a job description states "Bachelor's Degree in Business OR equivalent experience," a candidate with 5 years of relevant job titles (e.g., "Operations Specialist," "Account Manager") will score high on skill relevance and bypass filtering.   
