Executive synthesis

For a high-performing 18-year-old in Ireland, the evidence does not support a universal “university vs work” answer.

The strongest conclusion from the data is more specific:

The economic value of education depends heavily on what it is attached to. A degree leading to a regulated profession or a high-demand technical pathway can create substantial long-run option value. A generic degree with weak labour-market alignment and no work experience can have a much less compelling 10-year payoff. Conversely, direct workforce entry can be financially powerful in the first five years, but its long-term value depends on whether the person is accumulating portable skills, credentials, responsibility and managerial/technical scope, rather than simply tenure.

There is also a third route that is structurally important in Ireland: earn-and-learn apprenticeships, including Level 7/8 programmes. Current programmes include Level 8 cybersecurity, Level 8 financial-services analytics and degree-level accounting technology, alongside Level 7 programmes in areas such as supply chain and engineering. Apprentices are employees and earn a salary while studying.

For a 10-year horizon, I would therefore think of the choices as:

A. Credential-first → delayed earnings, potentially higher ceiling

B. Experience-first → immediate earnings, potentially lower ceiling unless deliberately managed

C. Credential + experience simultaneously → highest structural optionality where a good apprenticeship exists

The 18–28 financial model
First, an important statistical caveat

There is no official Irish statistic saying “the graduate overtakes the non-graduate at age X.”

The HEA/CSO system does have longitudinal graduate earnings data, linking higher-education records to Revenue earnings.

But that is not the same thing as a controlled longitudinal experiment comparing:

identical 18-year-olds → university vs direct employment → earnings at every subsequent age.

Eurostat's recent-graduate indicator is also primarily an employment-outcome measure, rather than an Irish 10-year earnings crossover dataset. In 2025, EU recent graduates with tertiary education had an 87% employment rate versus 77.2% for medium education, but that does not establish a particular earnings crossover age.

So any precise crossover age below is a modelled estimate, not an official CSO finding.

A. Baseline financial assumptions

I'll use 2026 euros / real purchasing-power terms for the main comparison, rather than pretending we can know today's €40,000 will literally be €40,000 in 2036.

