What this means

On a pure 18–28 cash-flow basis, Path B and particularly Path C can look substantially better.

That is not surprising.

Path A is effectively purchasing an asset — the qualification — while sacrificing four years of earnings.

The graduate is therefore making a capital investment in future earning capacity.

The mistake would be to conclude:

“The graduate loses €150k, therefore university is financially irrational.”

The correct question is:

Does the degree materially alter the person's earnings trajectory between roughly 25 and 40?

That is where the analysis changes.

4. The crossover question

The Irish evidence gives us several important clues.

At age 25 in the CSO's Growing Up in Ireland cohort, people with NFQ Level 7+ had median weekly employment income of €612 versus €484 for those below Level 7 — roughly a 26% difference.

And CSO's broader earnings data show the enormous age gradient:


15–24: €374.47 median weekly

25–29: €685.58

30–39: €846.23

40–49: €892.30.
The HEA/CSO longitudinal work also finds that earnings rise substantially with years after graduation.

My modelled interpretation

For a typical non-regulated corporate/technical pathway:

Annual salary crossover: approximately 24–27.

Cumulative cash-flow crossover: commonly later than 28, and potentially much later.

That distinction is crucial.

Someone can be earning more annually at 25 while still having earned less cumulatively because they sacrificed four years of income.

High-demand degree

For ICT, engineering, medicine, pharmacy, etc., annual salary crossover can occur earlier or the long-term gap can become substantially larger.

The HEA's 2021 data, for example, found younger ICT graduates were the highest-earning graduate field, followed by engineering/manufacturing/construction.

Weakly aligned degree

For an arts/general degree leading to a low-paid occupation, the crossover may be substantially later — or the graduate premium may never be large enough to compensate for the opportunity cost over a short horizon.

Confidence: Medium on direction; Low on a precise crossover year.

There is simply no responsible basis for presenting “age 25.4” as an official Irish statistic.

