The second candidate now has employer-verified evidence.

That changes recruitment.

Skills-first hiring is becoming more common: LinkedIn reports that in OECD-country recruiter searches, skills filters account for a much larger share than degree filters, although this is LinkedIn's own platform data rather than an Irish government statistic.

LinkedIn also reports increasing use of skills-based hiring and internal mobility frameworks.

The practical transition

At age 18:

Credential is a major signal.

At age 22:

Credential + internships + achievements.

At age 25:

Experience + achievements + skills + references increasingly dominate.

At age 30:

Scope, results, leadership, specialist expertise and network become increasingly important.

The degree never becomes irrelevant — but its marginal signalling value declines as direct evidence accumulates.

Confidence: Medium-high.

10. AI changes the equation

This is arguably the most important reason I would not recommend a generic “just get an office job” strategy in 2026.

The ILO's latest GenAI analysis finds clerical occupations remain the most exposed to generative AI. Particularly exposed occupations include data-entry clerks, typists, accounting/bookkeeping clerks and administrative secretaries.

The World Economic Forum likewise expects various clerical and administrative roles to decline through 2030, while software, cybersecurity, management, project and other higher-value roles grow.

Ireland is unusually exposed because of its concentration of digitally intensive multinational sectors.

The Irish Government's EGFSN reported that AI-related jobs and usage had more than doubled since 2023, with particularly strong demand for AI skills.

And the 2026 EGFSN assessment says AI is changing rather than simply eliminating demand for digital specialists, while routine entry-level tasks are particularly susceptible to automation.

