17. The Leaving Cert high-points trap

This deserves special attention for high-performing students.

A student gets 590 points.

Then everyone says:

“You could do medicine.”

Or:

“You should go to Trinity.”

Or:

“Why would someone with those points do a Level 7?”

This creates a subtle cognitive error:

Confusing academic ability with optimal career strategy.

High Leaving Cert performance tells us:

This person can succeed academically.

It does not tell us:

This person should maximise academic credentials.

Those are different questions.

The HEA data themselves show that Leaving Cert points are associated with later earnings, but the institution analysis also demonstrates that subject choice and student characteristics explain a substantial portion of apparent institutional differences.

A high-achieving student should therefore ask:

“What career asset am I trying to build?”

rather than:

“What is the highest-points course I can get into?”

