25. Core assumptions behind the financial model

The model should not be treated as a personal financial forecast.

It assumes:


The person remains continuously employable.

No prolonged unemployment.

No major recession.

No emigration.

No career-changing postgraduate degree.

Salaries are expressed approximately in 2026 purchasing power.

Tax rules are broadly similar over the modelling period.

The university student completes the four-year degree.

The direct entrant actually receives promotions rather than remaining in an entry-level position.

The apprenticeship is genuinely high quality and the employer invests in the apprentice.

No inheritance, house purchase, family business or entrepreneurial windfall is included.

Personal living costs are excluded from the central comparison except for the incremental cost of university, because otherwise we would be mixing the cost of being an adult with the cost of the career pathway.
Overall confidence in the exact € amounts: Low–Medium.

Confidence in the structural relationships: High.

Bottom line

If this were an actual executive career-planning exercise, I would not rank A, B and C universally.

I would instead use this decision rule:

Choose A when:

The destination requires the credential, or the degree is strongly aligned with a high-value profession and the course provides meaningful work experience.

Choose B when:

The target occupation is demonstrably skills/results-driven and the employer offers a credible progression ladder.

But B should come with a written five-year development plan, not simply “I'll work hard and get promoted.”

Choose C when:

A genuinely good Level 7/8 apprenticeship exists in the target field.

That is particularly attractive in:


cybersecurity

financial services

accounting

supply chain/operations

engineering/technical fields

selected digital roles
because it combines the two scarce assets of early adulthood:

time + experience

And that is ultimately the central insight of the 10-year analysis:

The real strategic enemy isn't university, and it isn't starting work at 18. It is spending the years from 18–28 accumulating only one form of career capital.

A graduate who accumulates degree + internships + technical skills + network can be extremely well positioned.

A non-graduate who accumulates experience + measurable achievements + technical skills + portable credentials + network can also be extremely well positioned.

The most vulnerable person is the one who has neither a strong credential nor strong evidence of increasing professional value by their mid-20s.

And in an AI-intensive Irish labour market, that final distinction is becoming more important, not less.

